hi there steve coffin here today i want  to talk about learning  using lists  a very common practice in language  instruction  remember if you enjoy these  videos about language learning please  subscribe to my channel  most learning books or many of them will  start with lists for example  i mentioned earlier that i bought this  living language series  and  they not only start with lists they  continue to use lists as far as i can  tell throughout their  uh  you know throughout these books so they  start out with the sounds of the  language that's the list of course the  greek letters is a list then we have  colors  parts of the body  rooms in the house members of your  family numbers  i mean it just goes on fruits and  vegetables everything is a list it  almost doesn't matter which page you  turn to there's a list adjectives are a  list and of course uh grammar you know  present tense different verb types  conjugations uh  demonstrative pronouns it doesn't matter  what it is it's all presented as a list  i find it very difficult to learn from a  list  trying to learn all the colors at once  is much more difficult than gradually  getting used to  green grass blue sky  in other words the use of these  terms  in context similarly you know names of  countries  uh  you know runes in the house it doesn't  matter uh  lists in my opinion  uh are not for learning lists are for  reference  so  once i have been exposed to enough of  the language and i'll explain how i  expose myself to the language  then at some point i may want to see a  list of  colors or  uh vegetables or  conjugation tables or something just to  kind of refresh myself to confirm or to  review  and so but you don't need to buy  a learning system like living language  for that the only advantage with living  language is that they have an online  version  where you have text-to-speech  but if i google uh parts of the body in  greek colors in greek numbers in greek  uh whatever it might be i'll find that  list and increasingly i'll find that  those lists with text-to-speech to help  me with the pronunciation if i need that  so  lists are for reference not for learning  uh  one of the best ways to become familiar  to create that familiarity where you are  kind of grazing and going over the same  material uh  in different situations different  contexts  meeting the same words over and over  again gradually reinforcing your grasp  on all of this one of the best things  i've seen  is those mini stories that i've  mentioned before pyotr at  realpolis.pl developed for learning  polish a hundred many stories each story  consists of a you know  he  got up in the morning he made coffee  brush his teeth and he went to work same  story then told by that person i get up  i do this i do that and then questions  and that's very reinforcing so  i enjoyed using that for polish now as a  result i have commissioned someone to  write stories in english  and these stories are going to feature  different aspects of the structure of  the language we're going to focus on the  more common verbs i think nouns you  can't really control but at least the  most common verbs in different tenses  and different grammatical situations and  the stories are written with a certain  amount of humor  we hope to have eventually a hundred of  them  um we have  15 now  uh in the english library at link with  audio  and i have also now had them translated  into greek  by the internet outsourced and i'm  having them recorded  now even without the audio i have  started using these for greek and i tell  you it's tremendous it's tremendous and  because for gree for me greek it's all  new words there are very few words there  that i so far that i can identify as as  words of greek origin that i've  encountered in other languages very few  it's mostly  stone  cold new vocabulary  uh but so these stories that i'm working  through the 15 that we do have and we're  getting another 15.  i find it very good and what makes it  even better  is using you know we have now at link we  have these four  activities we went away from just flash  guarding so that we have on a random  basis we have these four activities  flashcards  fill in the blank multiple choice and  dictation dictation you hear the  text-to-speech and you have to you have  to then type it out  and  it's fun to do because it's random and i  think randomizing things let's call it  gamification call it randomizing keeps  things fresh  and and i think one of the problems with  reviewing flashcards one after another  or doing lists one after another is that  that the brain requires yes repetition  but also requires novelty as manfred  spitzer the german neuroscientist  pointed out so keeping it random  introduces that element of novelty  otherwise you're getting less and less  fresh every time you're doing the same  thing over and over again  just as  it's very difficult with a list to be as  as focused on the 10th item on that list  as you were on the first item on that  list so all of this to say that  i have seen this overwhelming tendency  to teach things using lists i think it's  counterproductive i think the list  should be made available as a reference  and that learning as stephen krashen  phil rightly points out has to be based  on compelling input compelling content  and eventually that compelling content  is authentic content of genuine interest  but at the level where i am in greek uh  you know i tried importing an article  from a newspaper in greek into link and  it's just too painful to go through word  after word after word that i don't know  uh so you need something simpler  and that's where these stories come in  and if they're written with a little bit  of humor  and with enough repetition it's  something that you can  basically grab a hold of  build up your competence build up your  vocabulary build up your familiarity  with the structures  occasionally review these in lists but  fundamentally spend your time listening  and reading and eventually  hopefully i can catapult mouse catapult  myself into a situation where i can  access authentic content in greek i will  keep you posted thank you for listening  bye for now  [Music]  you
 

